{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0dda4fc2f71869d60169bb22d3df0c8191bb81d12cff887b7d", "created": 1773871827, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Patient Support System: Friends, Family Members, and Peers\",\"summary\":\"This community describes a patient-centered support network used during diagnosis and treatment, organized around the broader concept of a SUPPORT SYSTEM composed of FRIENDS, FAMILY MEMBER(s), and PEERS. The relationships emphasize practical and emotional assistance: FRIENDS may accompany patients to doctor visits and help capture information; FAMILY MEMBER(s) are highlighted as providers of transportation and emotional support and also as clinically relevant to risk (family history); and the SUPPORT SYSTEM is operationalized through \\\"enlisting support\\\" and concrete supports like transportation and meals. Overall, the community is oriented toward care access, day-to-day burden reduction, and shared decision support rather than any adversarial or illicit activity. [Data: Entities (951, 733, 2892, 934, 952); Relationships (1420, 1437, 1424, 5917, 5357,+more)]\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"SUPPORT SYSTEM is the central organizing entity connecting people and practical supports\",\"explanation\":\"SUPPORT SYSTEM is defined as the group of people around a patient (including friends, family, and peers) who provide practical help and emotional support, with roles that can vary by task and change over time. This makes it the conceptual hub of the community, framing other entities as contributors or mechanisms within a coordinated network of care assistance. [Data: Entities (951)]\\n\\nThe relationships reinforce SUPPORT SYSTEM’s centrality by explicitly linking it to transportation (a key practical support) and to “enlisting support,” which describes the act of arranging help from friends, family, or peers for transportation, meals, and emotional support. Together, these links describe both what the system provides and how it is activated. [Data: Relationships (1437, 5917); Entities (3019, 951)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Friends provide appointment-level support, including accompaniment and note-taking at doctor visits\",\"explanation\":\"FRIENDS are characterized as trusted personal contacts who provide both practical and emotional assistance during diagnosis and treatment, including transportation help, meals, and emotional support. They may also accompany patients to medical visits, assist with taking notes, and help manage day-to-day needs. [Data: Entities (733)]\\n\\nA specific relationship highlights that FRIENDS can accompany the patient to DOCTOR VISITS and help by taking notes during the appointment, positioning friends as a practical safeguard for information capture and emotional presence during clinical interactions. This function can influence patient understanding and follow-through by improving recall of clinician guidance. [Data: Relationships (1420); Entities (733)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Family members play dual roles: clinical risk context and hands-on logistical/emotional support\",\"explanation\":\"FAMILY MEMBER is described in two distinct roles. First, family history (having a close family member with prostate cancer) is noted as a factor that increases an individual’s likelihood of developing the disease. Second, family members are recommended as part of the support system to accompany patients to appointments, take notes, help the patient remember questions and information, assist with decision-making, and provide logistics like transportation and meals alongside emotional support. [Data: Entities (2892)]\\n\\nThe relationship data further specifies family members as possible providers of transportation and emotional support. These links operationalize the broader description by identifying concrete supportive actions (driving/arranging rides and providing emotional support) that family members can deliver in the care journey. [Data: Relationships (5357, 5359); Entities (2892, 2894, 2895)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Peers extend support beyond family and friends as members of the broader support system\",\"explanation\":\"PEERS are defined as non-family individuals (e.g., fellow patients, support-group peers, community members) who can become part of a person’s broader support system during diagnosis and treatment. They may provide both emotional encouragement and practical assistance, including logistics like transportation and meals, and general day-to-day support that complements family support. [Data: Entities (934)]\\n\\nA direct relationship ties PEERS to the SUPPORT SYSTEM, framing peers as eligible contributors within the patient’s wider network of assistance. This indicates the community is not limited to household relationships and can incorporate experience-based or community-based support. [Data: Relationships (1424); Entities (934, 951)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Transportation support is a key practical function of the support system and family involvement\",\"explanation\":\"TRANSPORTATION is explicitly identified as one of the support system’s key practical supports, intended to ensure reliable access to appointments and to manage travel and scheduling logistics during diagnosis and treatment. This highlights transportation as a potential bottleneck the community aims to reduce. [Data: Relationships (1437); Entities (951)]\\n\\nPROVIDING TRANSPORTATION is described as a supportive action where friends, family, peers, or others help the patient travel to medical appointments and treatments, including arranging rides and accompanying the patient as needed. Family members are specifically identified as possible helpers who can provide transportation, reinforcing their role in appointment adherence and access to care. [Data: Entities (2894); Relationships (5357)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Meals and meal provision illustrate day-to-day burden reduction as a recurring support function\",\"explanation\":\"MEALS are defined as food-related practical assistance provided by a patient’s support system (friends, family members, or peers), helping reduce the burden of cooking and meal planning while coping with medical care. This positions meal support as a routine, tangible intervention that supports daily functioning during treatment. [Data: Entities (952)]\\n\\nA relationship links MEALS to PROVIDING MEALS as the activity that delivers meal support, indicating the community distinguishes between the resource/support category (meals) and the action that operationalizes it (providing meals). This supports a task-oriented view of caregiving contributions. [Data: Relationships (5937); Entities (952)]\"},{\"summary\":\"“Enlisting support” functions as the activation mechanism for coordinating help across the network\",\"explanation\":\"ENLISTING SUPPORT is defined as arranging help from friends, family, or peers for transportation, meals, and emotional support during diagnosis and treatment. This frames support not as passive availability but as an active coordination process—mobilizing the network to meet changing needs. [Data: Entities (3019)]\\n\\nThe SUPPORT SYSTEM’s relationship to ENLISTING SUPPORT indicates that building/using the support system is explicitly described as “enlisting help from others,” implying a deliberate step in care planning. This can affect how consistently the patient receives transportation, meals, and emotional support across the diagnosis/treatment timeline. [Data: Relationships (5917); Entities (951, 3019)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Emotional support is formalized as a supportive action with family members as identified providers\",\"explanation\":\"PROVIDING EMOTIONAL SUPPORT is defined as a supportive action provided by friends, family, or peers to help the patient cope during diagnosis and treatment, establishing emotional care as a recognized component of the overall support model rather than an incidental benefit. [Data: Entities (2895)]\\n\\nA relationship specifically identifies family members as possible helpers who can provide emotional support. In combination with the broader definitions of friends and peers providing emotional encouragement, the community depicts emotional support as distributed across multiple relationship types within the patient’s network. [Data: Relationships (5359); Entities (2892, 2895, 733, 934)]\"}],\"rating\":2.2,\"rating_explanation\":\"Impact severity is low because the community centers on benign, health-oriented social support activities (transportation, meals, appointment accompaniment, and emotional support) rather than harmful, illegal, or destabilizing behavior. [Data: Entities (951, 733, 2892, 934, 952); Relationships (1437, 1420, 5357, 5359, 5917)]\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1618, "prompt_tokens": 3206, "total_tokens": 4824, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}